Okay, so I understand this is somewhat of a niche thing, but over the years I've found more and more times where I have wanted a macro from `core::` or `std::` to have a callback. Just in case this is nonstandard terminology, what I mean by this is I'd like the macros to accept an an optional "parameter" which is another macro to call at the end of the called macro's expansion. What I would expect is something like this:
```rust
include! (
    file: "../external/foo.txt",
    callback: {
        name: my_magic_macro,
        prefix: {@prefix},
        suffix: {acc: [],},
    },
);
// One expansion step later:
my_magic_macro! {
    @prefix
    // contents of `../external/foo.txt` included here
    acc: [],
}
// Expansion continues as per the definition of `my_magic_macro`
```
Now sure, my biggest use case for this was more than a little bit silly (running setup for a Befunge interpreter that ran in `macro_rules!` macros), but other macros like `stringify!` would be nice to have this on so that I didn't have to keep accumulating things weirdly in `:tt`s. For example:
```rust
macro_rules! arbitrary {
    (@conv input: [], output: $output:tt) => {
        arbitrary!(@validate $output)
    };
    (@conv input: ['_ $($rest:tt)*], output: [$($($output:literal)+)?]) => {
        arbitrary!(@conv input: [$($rest)*] output: [$($($output)+, )? " "])
    };
    (@conv input: [$head:ident $($rest:tt)*], output: $output:tt) => {
        stringify! {
            tts: [$head],
            callback: [
                name: arbitrary,
                prefix: [@conv @cb input: [$($rest)*], output: $output, new: ],
                suffix: [],
            ],
        }
    };
    (@conv @cb input: $input:tt, output: [$($($output:tt)+)?], new: $new:literal) => {
        arbitrary!(@conv input: $input, output: [$($($output)+, )? $new])
    };
    (@conv input: [$err:tt $($rest:tt)*], output: $output:tt) => {
        compile_error!(concat!(
            "Input may only be identifiers or `'_`! Found: `",
            stringify!($err),
            "`",
        ));
    };
    (@validate [$($lit:literal)*]) => {
        println!(concat!($($lit),*));
    };
    (@validate $other:tt) => {
        compile_error!("Unreachable!");
    };
    ($($input:tt)*) => {
        arbitrary!(@conv input: [$($input)*], output: [])
    };
}
```
And yes, I know that there's much better ways to accomplish this one in particular, but I hope to illustrate my point with the `@validate $other:tt` branch. Or if that's not particularly illustrative, maybe one could imagine a case where you `include!("../data/data.hex")` with a callback into a macro that reformats the hex data into a bunch of `const`s. As of right now that's not possible, since `some_macro!(include!("file"))`will only ever see `include!("file")` as its input, and never the actual contents of the file.

Some notes:
- I'm aware that there exist crates for eager evaluation of macros like these by way of proc macro shenanigans (read: scanning the input tokens for `include!` or `env!` or the like and replacing those segments with the expected outputs of such macro invocations), but I have never found that to be particularly satisfying.
- I have actually attempted to implement exactly this myself (see the `#macros` channel in the Rust Community Discord server), however there's one massive sticking point: `module_path!`. That's the only one that I am certain would need direct changes to `rustc` for if the user doesn't want a proc macro that re-does part of Rust's compilation phase just to sort out what the module path of the call site is.

Is there any interest in having such a feature implemented? I'd like to have it enough that I'd do that myself, but I would rather avoid the trouble if that's not the case.

