619521048.en:
i have ran into really weird behavior from the borrow checker when trying to make a macro that disallows unwinding :
```rs
struct UnwindingPreventer<'a>(&'a NoUnwindingChecker);
impl Drop for UnwindingPreventer<'_> {fn drop(&mut self) {}}
struct NoUnwindingChecker;
impl Drop for NoUnwindingChecker {fn drop(&mut self) {}}

macro_rules! nouninwind_replace {
    (&mut $x:ident = $e:expr => $b:block) => {{
        let r : &mut _ = $e;
        let p = ::core::ptr::from_mut(r);
        let $x = unsafe { core::ptr::read(p) };
        static GLOBAL : NoUnwindingChecker = NoUnwindingChecker;
        let mut unwinding_preventer = UnwindingPreventer(&GLOBAL);
        let checker = NoUnwindingChecker;
        unwinding_preventer.0 = &checker;
        let out = {
            $b
        };
        core::mem::forget(unwinding_preventer);
        unsafe { ::core::ptr::write(p, out); }
    }}
}


// compiles
pub fn good_id(mutably_borrowed : &mut Box<u32>) {
    nouninwind_replace!(&mut b = mutably_borrowed => {
        b
    });
}

// doesn't compile
pub fn bad_id(mutably_borrowed : &mut Box<u32>) {
    nouninwind_replace!(&mut b = mutably_borrowed => {
        match b {c => c}
    });
}
```
here `good_id` compiles while `bad_id` doesn't. this is weird and completely unexpected to me, and the people i have talked to about this @**theemathas**  and @**Daniel Henry-Mantilla|232018**  have not been able to figure it out either.

it is normal and expected that any function call or destructor that could happen inside the block would trigger a lifetime error, as it would add an unwinding branch and the drop order in the unwinding would lead to `UnwindingPreventer` being dangling when its destructor is called.

but when we i write `match b {c => c}` there is no function call, and there is no destructor, and if we transmute the lfetime to make it compile we do see there is no unwinding branch : https://rust.godbolt.org/z/h3axjazex.

without any unwinding branch, there should be no reason for the lifetimes to fail. so i am quite confused.

and i'd like to know if anyone has a clue what may be happening

619521048.zh:
我在尝试编写一个禁止展开的宏时，遇到了借用检查器非常诡异的行为：
```rs
struct UnwindingPreventer<'a>(&'a NoUnwindingChecker);
impl Drop for UnwindingPreventer<'_> {fn drop(&mut self) {}}
struct NoUnwindingChecker;
impl Drop for NoUnwindingChecker {fn drop(&mut self) {}}

macro_rules! nouninwind_replace {
    (&mut $x:ident = $e:expr => $b:block) => {{
        let r : &mut _ = $e;
        let p = ::core::ptr::from_mut(r);
        let $x = unsafe { core::ptr::read(p) };
        static GLOBAL : NoUnwindingChecker = NoUnwindingChecker;
        let mut unwinding_preventer = UnwindingPreventer(&GLOBAL);
        let checker = NoUnwindingChecker;
        unwinding_preventer.0 = &checker;
        let out = {
            $b
        };
        core::mem::forget(unwinding_preventer);
        unsafe { ::core::ptr::write(p, out); }
    }}
}


// 可以编译
pub fn good_id(mutably_borrowed : &mut Box<u32>) {
    nouninwind_replace!(&mut b = mutably_borrowed => {
        b
    });
}

// 无法编译
pub fn bad_id(mutably_borrowed : &mut Box<u32>) {
    nouninwind_replace!(&mut b = mutably_borrowed => {
        match b {c => c}
    });
}
```
这里 `good_id` 可以编译，但 `bad_id` 不行。这对我来说非常诡异，完全出乎意料；而且我向 @**theemathas** 和 @**Daniel Henry-Mantilla|232018** 咨询过，他们也没能弄清楚原因。

如果代码块内部可能发生函数调用或析构，那么触发生命周期错误是正常且符合预期的，因为这会增加一个展开分支，而展开时的析构顺序会导致调用 `UnwindingPreventer` 的析构函数时，它已经变成悬垂引用。

但是，当我写 `match b {c => c}` 时，这里既没有函数调用，也没有析构；而且如果我们通过转置生命周期让它编译，可以看到实际上并不存在展开分支：https://rust.godbolt.org/z/h3axjazex。

既然没有展开分支，生命周期就没有理由检查失败。因此我相当困惑。

想知道有没有人能看出这可能是怎么回事。

