617845353.en:
@_**Nia|571657** [said](https://rust-lang.zulipchat.com/#narrow/channel/219381-t-libs/topic/we.20have.20outpointers.20at.20home/near/617833644):
```quote
the folks over at #**t-lang/in-place-init** concluded that for this to enable a proper safe outptr api you then need a concept of unique lifetimes to enable one to then construct a proof-of-initialisation token from the `write` call which could be passed to do a "safe" `MaybeUninit::assume_init()` or in-place transmute or the likes
```

This unfortunately has probably crashed and burned. If we take a fairly normal "pass in outptr or nullptr to avoid that data generation" API:

```rust
fn foo<'a>(out: Option<WRef<'a, T>>) -> Proof<'a>;
```

then either you always return a proof token like this, or you map the `Option` into the return value as well which is redundant and unnecessary: the caller does know if they passed in Some or None. Either path you take, the caller can then do something horrible like this:

```rust
let (dup_some, dup_none) = if true {
  (Some(wref), None)
} else {
  (None, Some(wref))
};

let proof = foo(dup_none);
dup_some.unwrap().safe_prove_init(proof);
```

The `if true` trick allows the caller to create two `Option<Wref<'a, T>>`s with the unique lifetime brand duplicated, and therefore calling `foo(None)` gives a proof token that can be used to "initialise" the `Some(wref)` side.

I'm not sure there's a way to make this work :(

