@_**Nia|571657** [说](https://rust-lang.zulipchat.com/#narrow/channel/219381-t-libs/topic/we.20have.20outpointers.20at.20home/near/617833644)：
```quote
#**t-lang/in-place-init** 那边的人得出的结论是：要让这个机制支持一个真正安全的 outptr API，就需要一种“唯一生命周期”的概念，从而可以根据 `write` 调用构造出一个初始化证明令牌，然后把它传给“安全的” `MaybeUninit::assume_init()`、原地转置之类的操作
```

遗憾的是，这个方案大概已经胎死腹中了。如果我们采用一种相当常见的 API——传入 outptr 或 nullptr，以避免生成那份数据：

```rust
fn foo<'a>(out: Option<WRef<'a, T>>) -> Proof<'a>;
```

那么，要么你总是返回这样的证明令牌，要么也把 `Option` 映射到返回值中；后者既多余又没必要：调用者知道自己传入的是 Some 还是 None。无论走哪条路，调用者都可以做出下面这种可怕的事情：

```rust
let (dup_some, dup_none) = if true {
  (Some(wref), None)
} else {
  (None, Some(wref))
};

let proof = foo(dup_none);
dup_some.unwrap().safe_prove_init(proof);
```

`if true` 这个技巧允许调用者创建两个复制了唯一生命周期 brand 的 `Option<WRef<'a, T>>`，因此调用 `foo(None)` 会得到一个证明令牌，而这个令牌可以用来“初始化”`Some(wref)` 那一侧。

我不确定这是否有办法实现 :(

617848653.en:
If `WRef` still works equivalently to a `&mut` with reborrowing then no: once you reborrow and pass a `WRef` copy into a function, the original `WRef` is disabled until the function return value is dropped (if it captures the `'a` lifetime).

eg.
```rust
fn foo(a: &mut u32) -> &u32 {
  a
}

fn main() {
  let a = &mut 0u32;
  let b = foo(a);
  core::ptr::eq(a, b); // err: can't use a because it is still borrowed in b
}
```

617848653.zh:
如果 `WRef` 在重新借用方面仍然等价于 `&mut`，那就不行：一旦你重新借用，并把 `WRef` 的副本传给一个函数，原本的 `WRef` 就会被禁用，直到函数的返回值被丢弃（如果返回值捕获了 `'a` 生命周期）。

例如：
```rust
fn foo(a: &mut u32) -> &u32 {
  a
}

fn main() {
  let a = &mut 0u32;
  let b = foo(a);
  core::ptr::eq(a, b); // err: can't use a because it is still borrowed in b
}
```

617866832.en:
moving does not make memory uninitialized though. If it did, the conclusion to the UCG issue would not have been that this code is unsound because of the `*r2 = ...` statement:
```
pub fn drop_in_place_static<T>(r: &'static mut T) -> &'static mut MaybeUninit<T> {
    // Safety: r: 'static, so nobody else can ever use this reference again,
    // except through r2 (which assumes only that the referenced memory is a
    // `MaybeUninit<T>`), and all valid `T` are also valid `MaybeUninit<T>`.
    let r2: &'static mut MaybeUninit<T> = unsafe { core::mem::transmute(r) };

    // Safety: `r2` was transmuted from a reference to a `T`, so must be initialized.
    unsafe { r2.assume_init_drop(); }
    *r2 = MaybeUninit::uninit();
    r2
}
```

