617877914.en:
@_**Max|960514** [said](https://rust-lang.zulipchat.com/#narrow/channel/219381-t-libs/topic/we.20have.20outpointers.20at.20home/near/617876227):
```quote
How do you use `take_mut` to write uninit into `&mut i32`
```

you can't, since (per my reading) that would indeed be UB - just library UB. what I see as the semantics of `take_mut` is that we:
- move out of the `&mut T` (even if `T` isn't `Copy`), *which I would argue is semantically a proper move as if we'd moved out of an owned value, leaving the contents of the &mut logically uninit*;
- do something with the T;
- put the T back.

thus I'd argue that while the closure is executing, the &mut points to uninit since the value has been moved out of. the fact that it happens to achieve this with `ptr::read` is to me an implementation detail (one that's overly permissive, since `read` effectively creates a bitwise copy; what we *should* do here is move out of the raw pointer); especially if T is affine, moving out of it twice shouldn't produce a second valid T

