621896684.en:
Well, let me describe the proposal as I understand it anyway. I don't think it is written very clearly anywhere.

So a pointer consists of four parts:

```rust
struct Ptr {
    /// The runtime address of the pointer on the actual hardware
    cpu_addr: usize,
    /// On CHERI you also have capabilities that tell what memory you are allowed to access,
    /// at hardware runtime
    cpu_cap,
    /// The "plane of allocation" as it is called in the UCG thread, exists in the AM only,
    /// together with `cpu_addr` this identifies an offset in an allocation
    ghost_addr: Rational,
    /// The provenance as we know it, which determines whether it is UB to perform a read or write
    /// through the pointer
    ghost_cap,
}
```

The `ghost_addr` allows us to have as many "address space copies" in the AM as we want, so you never run out of address space. `addr()` simply gives you the hardware address:

```rust
impl Ptr {
    fn addr(self) -> usize {
        self.cpu_addr
    }
}
```

Equality checks both the hardware and the ghost address:

```rust
impl Eq for Ptr {
    fn eq(self, other: Ptr) -> bool {
        self.cpu_addr == other.cpu_addr
        && self.ghost_addr == other.ghost_addr
    }
}
```

No side effects, no NB, perfectly optimisable. But not the same as comparing the hardware addresses.

You can also do `Cmp`, just take the lexicographic or anti-lexicographic order on the pair `(cpu_addr, ghost_addr)`.

Only `expose_provanance()` is miserable... One reason is you want `ptr1.expose_provenance() == ptr2.expose_provenance()` to compare the pointers, not only addresses. The deeper reason is that `from_exposed_provenance()` becomes crazy otherwise. So what we do is that we force all exposed allocations to live in the same "plane", let's say plane `0`. If the pointed-to allocation is in a different plane, we just OOM.

```rust
impl Ptr {
    fn expose_provenance(self) -> usize {
        if self.ghost_addr != 0 {
            oom()
        }
        self.ghost_cap.expose(); // we have no idea what that does :(
        self.addr()
    }
}
```

Advantages:
* infinite address space, so you can remove dead allocs/deallocate early if no pointer to the allocation is ever passed to `expose_provenance()`
* `Ptr::addr()` and `Ptr::eq()` have no side effects, and don't really form an optimisation barrier in any way
* No NB, angelic choice or other questionable things
* No infinite integers or provenance on integers

Disadvantages:
* If you want to compare pointers, you have to compare the pointers, not their addresses

621896684.zh:
不过，我还是按自己的理解描述一下这个提案吧。我觉得目前没有哪个地方把它写得很清楚。

所以，一个指针由四个部分组成：

```rust
struct Ptr {
    /// 指针在实际硬件上的运行时地址
    cpu_addr: usize,
    /// 在 CHERI 上，你还会有用于说明在硬件运行时允许访问哪些内存的能力
    cpu_cap,
    /// UCG 讨论串中称为“分配平面”的东西，只存在于 AM 中；
    /// 它和 `cpu_addr` 一起标识分配中的某个偏移
    ghost_addr: Rational,
    /// 我们熟悉的 provenance，它决定通过该指针执行读写是否属于 UB
    ghost_cap,
}
```

`ghost_addr` 允许我们在 AM 中拥有任意多个“地址空间副本”，因此永远不会耗尽地址空间。`addr()` 只需返回硬件地址：

```rust
impl Ptr {
    fn addr(self) -> usize {
        self.cpu_addr
    }
}
```

相等性检查硬件地址和 ghost 地址：

```rust
impl Eq for Ptr {
    fn eq(self, other: Ptr) -> bool {
        self.cpu_addr == other.cpu_addr
        && self.ghost_addr == other.ghost_addr
    }
}
```

没有副作用，没有 NB，而且完全可以优化。但这和比较硬件地址不是一回事。

你也可以进行 `Cmp`，只需对二元组 `(cpu_addr, ghost_addr)` 采用字典序或反字典序。

只有 `expose_provanance()` 比较麻烦……原因之一是，你希望 `ptr1.expose_provenance() == ptr2.expose_provenance()` 比较的是指针，而不只是地址。更深层的原因是，否则 `from_exposed_provenance()` 会变得非常疯狂。所以我们的做法是，强制所有被暴露的分配都位于同一个“平面”中，比如说平面 `0`。如果目标分配位于其他平面，我们就直接 OOM。

```rust
impl Ptr {
    fn expose_provenance(self) -> usize {
        if self.ghost_addr != 0 {
            oom()
        }
        self.ghost_cap.expose(); // 我们完全不知道这会做什么 :(
        self.addr()
    }
}
```

优点：
* 无限地址空间，因此如果某个分配的指针从未传给 `expose_provenance()`，就可以消除无用分配，或提前释放分配
* `Ptr::addr()` 和 `Ptr::eq()` 没有副作用，也不会以任何方式真正构成优化障碍
* 没有 NB、天使式选择或其他可疑机制
* 没有无限大整数，也没有附着在整数上的 provenance

缺点：
* 如果你想比较指针，就必须比较指针本身，而不是比较它们的地址

622017933.en:
@_**niluxv|401145** [said](https://rust-lang.zulipchat.com/#narrow/channel/136281-t-opsem/topic/Do.20we.20already.20have.20overlapping.20allocations.3F.20.28OOM.20Edition.29/near/621933677):
```quote
> instead of allocation planes I just imagine all allocations having unique IDs

~~Yes, that is equivalent (but you need to fix a bijection between AllocIds and rationals except 0, to use for order comparisons).~~ Edit: with comparisons it can actually make a difference.
```

Ugh, with comparisons everything becomes subtle. I suppose we order anti-lexicographically, so first compare the plane, then the address. This feels more intuitive since if you take two pointers in the same allocation, there won't be pointers into a different allocation in-between.

The model which allows multiple allocations to live in the same plane allows strictly more behaviours than the one where there is at most 1 allocation per plane except for plane 0:

```rust
fn exhaust_plane_0() {
    let layout = Layout::new(1, 1);
    for i in 0..usize::MAX {
        let ptr: *mut u8 = alloc(layout).expose_provenance();
        assert!(!x.is_null());
    }
}

fn main() {
    exhaust_plane_0();
    let ptr1 = alloc();
    let plane1_low = ptr1.with_addr(0);
    let plane1_high = ptr1.with_addr(usize::MAX);
    let ptr2 = alloc();
    // can this print `true`?
    println!(ptr1_low <= ptr2 && ptr2 <= ptr1_high);
}
```

With multiple allocations per plane you could see the program print `true`. With one allocation per plane (the AllocId + forced bit approach) that can never happen.

622017933.zh:
@_**niluxv|401145** [说](https://rust-lang.zulipchat.com/#narrow/channel/136281-t-opsem/topic/Do.20we.20already.20have.20overlapping.20allocations.3F.20.28OOM.20Edition.29/near/621933677)：
```quote
> 我只是想象所有分配都有唯一 ID，而不是使用分配平面

~~是的，两者等价（但你需要在 AllocId 和除 0 以外的有理数之间确定一个双射，用于顺序比较）。~~ 编辑：加上比较后，情况确实可能有所不同。
```

唉，一涉及比较，一切就都变得微妙了。我想我们应该采用逆字典序，也就是先比较平面，再比较地址。这样感觉更直观，因为如果你取同一分配中的两个指针，那么它们之间不会夹着指向其他分配的指针。

允许多个分配位于同一平面的模型，比除了平面 0 外每个平面至多有一个分配的模型允许严格更多的行为：

```rust
fn exhaust_plane_0() {
