622111310.en:
You are talking past each other. But Ralf is correct that the optimisation does not apply to an arbitrary function-call instead of `println` (I'll give a proof). Whether it is allowed for `println` depends on how you spec `println`.

For clarity, let me just set a few definitions straight. Time-travelling-UB means that `trace + (out(x), UB)` and `trace + (UB)` are equivalent traces for every trace `trace` and value `x`. It has nothing to do with whether some function possibly diverges. At least that is how Connor is using the term, and how I will use the term. We assume we are in a semantics *with* time-travelling-UB.

The transformation we are talking about is
```rust
fn source(){
    let x: u8 = pick();
    println("{x}");
    if x==47 { unreachable_unchecked() }
    println!("bar");
}
```
to
```rust
fn target(){
    let x: u8 = pick();
    println("{x}");
    unreachable_unchecked();
}
```

The validity of that transform depends how you "implement"/spec `println`. Just saying it is non-`willreturn` is not a spec, but apparently whether `println` diverges is at the discretion of the environment. Obviously, `println!(x)` is also creating an observable event with the value of `x`. But you cannot create an observable event and ask the environment whether it wants to diverge at the same time. So either `println` is
```rust
fn println1(x) {
    if input() {
        loop {}
    }
    out(x);
}
```
(this would make sense if the OS puts you in a queue, and if it ever finds time for you then it passes `x` on to the environment but guarantees from that moment on that it will return to your program)
or
```rust
fn println2(x) {
    out(x);
    if input() {
        loop {}
    }
}
```
(the OS passes `x` on to the say the graphics driver, but then queues you again while it is waiting for the graphics pipeline to print `x` to the screen).

With `println1` the transformation is allowed, with `println2` it is not.

Proof for `println2`: let's say I'm the environment. I get to see your `x`. Next I'm asked for input, whether to make `println2` diverge. If `x` was 47 I say "diverge". Otherwise I say "return to the program". `source` has traces `(out(47), Diverge)` and `(out(anything other than 47), out("bar"))`. `target` has traces `(out(anything), UB)`. Clearly `target` does not refine `source`.

622111310.zh:
你们俩说的不是一回事。不过 Ralf 说得对：把任意函数调用换成 `println` 时，这个优化并不适用（我会给出证明）。它是否适用于 `println`，取决于你如何规定 `println` 的语义。

为避免歧义，我先把几个定义讲清楚。时间旅行 UB 意味着，对于任意轨迹 `trace` 和值 `x`，`trace + (out(x), UB)` 与 `trace + (UB)` 是等价轨迹。这与某个函数是否可能发散没有任何关系。至少 Connor 使用这个术语时是这个意思，而我也会按这个意思使用。我们假设所处的是一种*具有*时间旅行 UB 的语义。

我们讨论的变换是将
```rust
fn source(){
    let x: u8 = pick();
    println("{x}");
    if x==47 { unreachable_unchecked() }
    println!("bar");
}
```
变成
```rust
fn target(){
    let x: u8 = pick();
    println("{x}");
    unreachable_unchecked();
}
```

这个变换是否有效，取决于你如何“实现”或规定 `println` 的语义。仅仅说它不是 `willreturn` 并不能算一种规范，但显然 `println` 是否发散是由环境决定的。很明显，`println!(x)` 也会创建一个携带 `x` 的可观察事件。但你不可能一边创建可观察事件，一边同时询问环境是否希望发散。因此，`println` 要么是
```rust
fn println1(x) {
    if input() {
        loop {}
    }
    out(x);
}
```
（如果操作系统先把你放进队列，而一旦有时间处理你，就将 `x` 传给环境，并保证从那一刻起会返回到你的程序中，这种定义就是合理的）

要么是
```rust
fn println2(x) {
    out(x);
    if input() {
        loop {}
    }
}
```
（操作系统先将 `x` 传给，比如说，图形驱动程序，但随后在等待图形流水线打印 `x` 到屏幕上的期间，又把你放回队列中）。

对于 `println1`，这个变换是允许的；对于 `println2`，则不允许。

下面证明 `println2` 的情况：假设我是环境。我可以看到你的 `x`。接着系统询问我是否要让 `println2` 发散。如果 `x` 是 47，我就回答“发散”；否则我就回答“返回程序”。`source` 的轨迹包括 `(out(47), Diverge)` 和 `(out(anything other than 47), out("bar"))`。`target` 的轨迹则是 `(out(anything), UB)`。显然，`target` 并不精化 `source`。

