```

I realised I'm probably wrong about this: if we create a `&Cell` by projecting into a field of `&StructContainingCell`, go out of bounds from that and mutate a non-`Cell` field, and then check that through the `&StructContainingCell` then obviously if a non-`Cell` fields value has changed behind a `&` reference then UB has happened. ie. indeed the opsem question of whether `&Cell` gains R/W rights to neighbouring memory does not actually affect the optimisability of reading a non-`Cell` field twice on boths sides of an opaque call.

```rust
struct StructContainingCell {
  a: usize,
  b: Cell<usize>,
}

fn method(s: &StructContainingCell) {
  if s.a !== 0 {
    return;
  }
  // &Cell<usize> sent to some opaque call - opsem wise it has RW rights to `s.a`
  opaque_call(&s.b);
  // SAFETY: if this fails then UB has happened - `&StructContainingCell` lives for the duration of `opaque_call` and therefore mutation of `a` would violate &-reference invariants.
  unsafe { assert_unchecked(s.a == 0) };
}
```

