623524576.en:
@_**Jake Goulding|116155** [said](https://rust-lang.zulipchat.com/#narrow/channel/122651-general/topic/Is.20it.20conceptually.20possible.20to.20.22restore.22.20an.20.26own.20reference.3F/near/623157536):
```quote
Of course, it's also possible that my base understanding of `&own` references is off-track and so this question is ultimately nonsense. :wink:
```

As mentioned above, the easiest way to "solve" your question is through a `{ person.name }` temporary flowing out and back into the `person.name` place.

With that being said, your intuition is very close to another topic, where here it might be "overkill" (it would just be about saving on a (bit-)*moving* as `String`, but which is definitely a theoretically interesting question, and even more so, a potentially crucial aspect for `in-place-init`: the notion of what has been called "**notarization**": the idea is that the `&own` reference could have not *two*, but *three* generic parameters/parts:
1. the type of the referee,
2. the (covariant/shrinkable) lifetime of the borrow, which stipulates when the borrow may have expired. We can always "pessimize" this limit, which is why the lifetime of (any) borrow is allowed to shrink.
3. And finally, a fully unique *identification* of the *place* being `&own` used, in and of itself: the exact/precise "origin/provenance" of the borrow.

In pseudo-code: `&'r own<'{person.name}> String`.

Now, if `intensive_work()` were made generic over this identity (which could be deemed as working similarly (albeit orthogonally) to lifetimes, except for their invariance, we'd have:

```rust
fn intensive_work<place 'P>(name: &'_ own<'P> String) -> Result<bool, &'_ own<'P> String> {
    if false { Ok(true) } else { Err(name) }
}
```

From there:

```rust
fn cool_things(person: Person) {
    match intensive_work(&own person.name) {
        Ok(v) => println!("Did hard work: {v}"),
        Err(rejected) => {
            person.name <- rejected; // Notarize it back.
            some_alternate(person); // `person` is fully initialized here
        }
    }
}
```

---

@**Jake Goulding** see #**t-lang/in-place-init>out-pointers using place generics**  for (a lot :woozy_face:) more info about these musings

